  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of various websites and contact information for different offices and resources related to the United States House of Representatives and the United States Senate. The House of Representatives websites include the main website at house.gov and the restricted HouseNet portal at housenet.house.gov, which provides access to \"House Offices Only\" websites such as the Clerk of the House, Legislative Resource Center, and Legislative Counsel. These resources offer assistance with archiving records, ordering publications, and access to current and historical House documents.\n\nThe Senate websites include the main website at senate.gov and the restricted SenateWeb portal at senate.gov. These resources provide access to various Senate offices, committees, and services, including the Secretary of the Senate, Senate IT, and the Senate Library. The report also mentions Congress.gov, a public legislative information website that offers timely, accurate, and complete legislative information from the 93rd Congress (1973-present).\n\nAdditionally, the report lists various external resources such as the Congressional Research Service, Government Accountability Office, and the Government Publishing Office, which offer research, analysis, and publication services to congressional staff. These resources can be accessed through their respective websites or by contacting them directly. Overall, the report serves as a comprehensive guide to the various resources and contact information available to support the work of the United States Congress.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of various websites and contact information for different offices and resources related to the United States House of Representatives and the United States Senate. The House of Representatives websites include the main website at house.gov and the restricted HouseNet portal at housenet.house.gov, which provides access to \"House Offices Only\" websites such as the Clerk of the House, Legislative Resource Center, and Legislative Counsel. These resources offer assistance with archiving records, ordering publications, and access to current and historical House documents.\n\nThe Senate websites include the main website at senate.gov and the restricted SenateWeb portal at senate.gov. These resources provide access to various Senate offices, committees, and services, including the Secretary of the Senate, Senate IT, and the Senate Library. The report also mentions Congress.gov, a public legislative information website that offers timely, accurate, and complete legislative information from the 93rd Congress (1973-present).\n\nAdditionally, the report lists various external resources such as the Congressional Research Service, Government Accountability Office, and the Government Publishing Office, which offer research, analysis, and publication services to congressional staff. These resources can be accessed through their respective websites or by contacting them directly. Overall, the report serves as a comprehensive guide to the various resources and contact information available to support the work of the United States Congress.",
  "score": 24.719100673084217,
  "answers": [
    "This report provides a brief list of key House, Senate, legislative support agencies, and executive branch offices, as well as links to online resources of use to new congressional staff who work with legislative procedures and conduct legislative research. Some of the websites listed are available only to congressional offices; other sites are restricted by chamber and are only available to those staff working in either House or Senate offices. This report is intended for congressional use only and will be updated annually."
